166534423828125, 0.0249481201171875, 0.00693511962890625, 0.00460052490234375, 0.038299560546875, -0.01371002197265625, 0.00197601318359375, -0.01024627685546875, -0.0012531280517578125, 0.03717041015625, 0.0411376953125, -1.4841556549072266e-05, 0.0004684925079345703, -0.0181732177734375, -0.0165252685546875, 0.01739501953125, -0.01552581787109375, 0.0162353515625, 0.004261016845703125, -0.03875732421875, -0.0112152099609375, 0.01483154296875, -0.0011396408081054688, 0.0127410888671875, 0.0450439453125, -0.014251708984375, -0.016265869140625, -0.00634002685546875, 0.0019741058349609375, -0.0098724365234375, 0.002788543701171875, 0.00536346435546875, -0.01418304443359375, -0.00940704345703125, 0.01549530029296875, -0.03155517578125, -0.005157470703125, -0.002132415771484375, 0.006450653076171875, 0.0303802490234375, -0.0187835693359375, -0.003376007080078125, 0.00855255126953125, 0.0103607177734375, 0.01519012451171875, -0.052581787109375, -0.0205230712890625, 0.005283355712890625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Delegating cognitive operations to generative artificial intelligence (AI) raises important questions about governance and control: Who sets direction, and when? How much autonomy should AI systems receive\u2014and through which mechanisms do humans provide guidance? This article investigates when, how, and through what mechanisms humans guide AI work, and how these patterns change depending on modes of human-AI interaction.\n\n**Key Concepts and Definitions**\n\nWe distinguish between two principal modes of delegation, each defined by the timing and extent of human input. Table 1 below summarizes definitions, boundary rules, and examples, including elaborations on gray-area cases. (Supplementary Figure 1 in the data repository provides a detailed decision tree; Table S1 shows a sample breakdown.)\n\n| Mode                 | Core Definition                                                         | Boundary Rule                                                                              | Example(s)                                                                                               |\n|----------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Specified delegation | Human sets explicit goals or constraints up front; minimal feedback after initial instructions, before output is finalized. | If substantial feedback or adjustment occurs after generation begins, not considered purely specified. All prompt engineering before initial content generation is specified. | User submits a detailed prompt (\u201cSummarize X in 200 words, covering points A, B, C\u201d) and accepts the first summary without asking for changes. Tuning or optimizing prompts before clicking 'submit' (even through trial and error) also counts as specified delegation. Editing the prompt multiple times before hitting 'submit' is still specified delegation, as long as the AI has not yet produced intermediate or final outputs. |\n| Iterative coproduction | Human collaborates interactively, providing feedback or adjustments after initial AI output. | If user reviews and revises outputs interactively after creation has begun (i.e., after any AI content is generated), it is iterative coproduction regardless of initial instructions. | User submits a prompt, receives an AI-generated draft, then asks for specific changes (\u201cExpand section 2,\u201d \u201cChange the tone to more formal\u201d) and repeats this loop. Even if the initial prompt was highly specified, any cycle of output-review-and-revise is iterative coproduction. For example, after reviewing a generated paragraph, the user requests clarifications or corrections, triggering further AI edits.|\n\n**Resolving Gray Cases:** Any modification, experimentation, or refinement *before* the AI produces output\u2014regardless of how many times the user edits or resubmits the prompt\u2014is classified as specified delegation. All feedback, correction, or direction occurring *after* the AI generates content, whether for first or intermediate outputs, constitutes iterative coproduction.\n\n*Delegation* refers to assigning cognitive steps from human to machine. *Prior human direction* refers to human intent shaping the task before AI execution.\n\n**Research Questions**\n\nThis study asks:  \n1. At which stages are humans most influential in shaping AI output during task delegation?  \n2. How do delegation patterns differ between structured (API-based) and interactive (chat-based) environments?\n\n**Methodology**\n\nWe clarify the methodology by organizing key steps with subheadings for accessibility. Further sample breakdowns, decision tree logic, and statistical summaries are provided in Supplementary Table S1 and Table S2.\n\n1. **Sample and Data Preprocessing**\n   - **Dataset:** Anthropic Economic Index (AEI), comprising 2.1 million anonymized human-AI interactions (April\u2013May 2026), with fields for interaction process, task metadata, and basic user descriptors.\n   - **Sample characteristics:** ~80% U.S.-based, English-speaking knowledge workers (administration, technology, communications, business). Creative, manufacturing, and non-English tasks are underrepresented (see Table S1 for full sectoral breakdown).\n   - **Preprocessing:** Removal of incomplete records; standardization to O*NET level-0 task codes for most granular classification. Final analysis at node-month-task level to capture usage patterns.\n\n2. **Operationalization of \"Work Orientation\"**\n   - **Definition:** Work orientation captures the proportion of a user\u2019s AI tasks flagged as work/professional rather than personal in the AEI dataset.\n   - **Measurement:** For records with both work and personal usage data, \u201cwork orientation\u201d is calculated as the percent of tasks in a given node-month marked as work-related (range: 0 to 1).\n   - **Counterfactual Modeling:** We simulate a +10 percentage point shift (e.g., from 0.40 to 0.50) in work-related intent *per node-month*, holding all else constant. This is a synthetic intervention, using user-reported data as the base, not an observed behavioral shift.\n   - **Rationale:** This models how delegation would change if the average professional usage of generative AI rose in a given organizational unit or user cluster.\n\n3. **Modeling Approach and Statistical Analysis**\n   - **Regression Model:** Linear regressions estimate the association between the modeled 10% increase in work orientation and the prevalence of each delegation mode. Regressions include node and month fixed effects, plus covariates for task type (O*NET level-0), user sector, and monthly usage volume.\n   - **Sample restriction:** Only node-month pairs with both API and Claude.ai (chat) records are included (n=1,411), ensuring direct comparison (common support).\n   - **Outcome Variable:** For each delegation mode, a normalized delegation score is computed per node-month-task (mean=0, SD=1), representing the standardized frequency of each modal pattern.\n   - **Robustness:** Sensitivity checks include alternative specifications (logit, task complexity as covariate, stratification by user AI experience), as detailed in Table S2 and Supplementary Methods.\n   - **Statistical reporting:** Robust standard errors clustered by node. Full regression tables and robustness checks are summarized in Appendix Table A1.\n\n*Table A1: Key Regression Model Parameters (see Appendix for full details)*  \n| Variable                | Coefficient (API) | Coefficient (Claude.ai) | 95% CI              | Covariates        |\n|-------------------------|-------------------|-------------------------|---------------------|-------------------|\n| Work orientation (+10%) | 2.76 (specified), -0.30 (iterative) | 1.45 (specified), +0.15 (iterative) | [2.30, 3.22], etc. | Task type, sector, volume, FE |\n\n**Findings and Interpretation**\n\n- When work orientation is synthetically increased by 10 percentage points (from observed user data), **specified delegation** rises by 2.76 standard deviations (95% CI: [2.30, 3.22]) in API environments and 1.45 (95% CI: [0.93, 1.97]) in Claude.ai (chat). Practically, this means \u201chighly specified up-front instructions\u201d increase from median (~40% of tasks) to ~85%.\n- **Iterative coproduction** decreases slightly in API mode (-0.30 SD) but increases in Claude.ai (+0.15 SD), a statistically significant difference (gap: 0.45 SD; 95% CI: [0.15, 0.75]). Subgroup analyses (Table S2) show that administration and business experience larger increases in specified delegation, while creative and communication sectors see modest gains in iterative coproduction via chat.\n- **Gray Cases and Task Complexity:** Sensitivity analyses stratifying by user AI experience and task complexity find similar patterns, though less experienced users and highly ambiguous tasks lean more toward iterative coproduction in all interfaces.\n\nInterpretation: In professionalized settings, APIs channel users toward up-front, structured specification\u2014encouraging \u201cset and execute\u201d delegation and limiting feedback cycles. Chat modes (e.g., Claude.ai) foster collaboration, re-specification, and real-time oversight, especially when tasks are ambiguous or creative.\n\n**Limitations and Boundary Conditions**\n\nSeveral important constraints shape interpretation:\n\n- **Sample Representativeness:** The sample is skewed toward U.S. office professionals in select sectors.\n- **Non-experimental Design:** While fixed-effects regressions and sensitivity checks (including alternate task complexity controls and stratification by user AI experience) lend confidence, unobserved factors (such as prior AI familiarity and organizational context) may bias results. Further stratified analyses are suggested for future work.\n- **Effect Size Generalization:** Marginal effect estimates may not extrapolate to environments with radically different intent or delegation practices.\n- **Interface Self-selection:** Those who choose APIs may already exhibit highly specified work habits\u2014a factor addressed via fixed effects and robustness checks, but not fully eliminated.\n- **Applicability Scope:** Results pertain primarily to non-embodied, cognitive-oriented, generative AI settings; caution is warranted in extending to robotics or high-stakes domains.\n\n**Implications and Policy Recommendations**\n\nEmpirical findings directly inform design and governance:\n\n- **Finding:** APIs yield much higher up-front specification for work-related tasks.\n  - **Recommendation:** For routine or risk-sensitive work, require submission templates mandating detailed instructions for API-based systems; this mirrors observed gains in specification.\n- **Finding:** Chat interfaces support ongoing, real-time adjustments and user oversight, especially for open-ended or creative tasks.\n  - **Recommendation:** Embed real-time feedback checkpoints and easy revision prompts in chat UIs, aligning with observed increases in iterative coproduction.\n- **Finding:** Context and user expertise moderate delegation styles.\n  - **Recommendation:** Offer UI defaults tuned by role or context (e.g., \u201capproval required\u201d for high-stakes, \u201crapid prototyping\u201d for creative roles), and allow for switching delegation modes mid-task to match user and task needs.\n- **Finding:** Delegation mode variation underscores the need for flexible oversight.\n  - **Recommendation:** Policymakers should allocate structured human oversight at phases most prone to error or drift, incorporating delegation mode as a variable in risk assessment.\n\n**Accessibility and Supplementary Materials**\n\nFor transparency, the main text includes direct definitions, gray-area adjudication logic, and a"}}